This changes how the V1 corpus should be interpreted.

'''The overwhelming majority of V1 Main pages are evidence about BITwiki itself or about how BITwiki composes, navigates, validates, and governs knowledge. They are not 162 candidate encyclopedia articles waiting to be assigned entity types.'''

== First-class subject candidates ==

The current pass identifies two V1 pages whose historical artifact role is a coherent first-class subject/project object rather than BITwiki's own machinery.

=== Bioluminescent Organisms ===

The archived page is a composed subject page with its own overview, biochemical mechanism, examples, applications, related theories, Biology membership, and historical <code>Ideas</code> identity signal. Its child pages are transcluded into the parent.

Migration implication:

* preserve the parent as the canonicalization candidate;
* treat its historical subpages as composition evidence and source material;
* normalize the V1 <code>Idea</code> signal through the V2 concept/type review rather than copying the old label mechanically;
* preserve the relationship to Fluorescent Coral Reefs without inheriting the old relation wording uncritically.

Archive source: <code>archive-v1/pages/Main/Bioluminescent Organisms.mediawiki</code>.

=== Fluorescent Coral Reefs ===

The archived page is a composed implementation/project page with Overview, Genetic Engineering, Environmental Applications, and Implementation Challenges subpages. V1 itself contains an interesting identity tension: the page presentation calls it an implementation project while its API category membership records <code>Implementations</code>.

Migration implication:

* preserve it as a first-class canonicalization candidate;
* do not decide <code>Implementation</code> versus <code>Project</code> from one historical label;
* review the modeled object: a proposed engineered coral research/project system may have project, implementation, technology, and biological dimensions that should be represented through one primary modeled identity plus relationships;
* preserve uncertainty about feasibility and historical development status rather than inheriting presentation-era status language as epistemic fact.

Archive source: <code>archive-v1/pages/Main/Fluorescent Coral Reefs.mediawiki</code>.

== Book Matter is carrying real subject signal ==

The 52 composition artifacts are not disposable fragments.

Several contain genuine domain knowledge but were deliberately implemented as reusable units. Examples include:

{| class="wikitable"
! Historical artifact !! Composition signal !! Migration interpretation
|-
| <code>Emergence</code> || Concept definition fragment || Subject signal survives; historical artifact role is reusable definition.
|-
| <code>Feedback-loop</code> || Concept definition fragment || Subject signal survives; historical artifact role is reusable definition.
|-
| <code>Homeostasis</code> || Concept definition fragment || Subject signal survives; historical artifact role is reusable definition.
|-
| <code>Systems-analysis</code> || Method fragment || Strong evidence for a Method-like knowledge pattern, but the historical object is reusable methodology content.
|-
| <code>Peer-review-protocol</code> || Protocol fragment || Strong Protocol signal inside BITwiki governance/quality machinery.
|-
| <code>Validation-procedure</code> || Protocol fragment || Strong procedural signal inside epistemic validation machinery.
|-
| <code>Empirical-validation</code> || Evidence-summary fragment || Epistemic/evidence content rather than proof of a new entity type.
|-
| <code>Peer-review-summary</code> || Evidence-summary fragment || Review/evidence composition signal.
|}

This is exactly why V2 should preserve Book Matter as a first-class compositional idea.

A fragment can contain canonicalizable knowledge without the fragment itself becoming the canonical top-level object.

